% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Metals: base metals (Section XV note 3, rulings) and hallmarks of precious
% metal (the FTC guides; Chapter 71 note 5 reads them as alloys of
% precious metal)
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
"zinc alloy" is a word for the metal zinc alloy,
    as stated in the jewellery listing format, confer "Metals Type: Zinc Alloy".
"stainless steel" is a word for the metal stainless steel,
    as stated in ruling NY R03173, confer "European contemporary jewelry made of stainless steel".
"copper" is a word for the metal copper,
    as stated in HTSUS Section XV, confer "the expression \"base metals\" means: iron and steel, copper, nickel".
"brass" is a word for the metal brass,
    as stated in ruling NY N151556, confer "composed of brass and plated with silver and gold".
"titanium" is a word for the metal titanium,
    as stated in ruling NY N343561, confer "Titanium is considered a base metal".
"925" is a word for the metal silver alloy,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "unless it is at least 925/1,000ths pure silver".
"sterling" is a word for the metal silver alloy,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "Use of the words “solid silver,” “Sterling Silver,” “Sterling,” or the abbreviation “Ster.” to mark, describe, or otherwise represent all or part of an industry product unless it is at least 925/1,000ths pure silver".
"sterling silver" is a word for the metal silver alloy,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "Use of the words “solid silver,” “Sterling Silver,” “Sterling,” or the abbreviation “Ster.” to mark, describe, or otherwise represent all or part of an industry product unless it is at least 925/1,000ths pure silver".
"925 sterling" is a word for the metal silver alloy,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "unless it is at least 925/1,000ths pure silver".
"925,sterling" is a word for the metal silver alloy,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "unless it is at least 925/1,000ths pure silver".
"18k" is a word for the metal gold alloy,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "Such product may also be marked and described by a designation of the karat fineness of the gold alloy unaccompanied by the word “Gold” (for example, “14 Karat,” “14Kt.,” “14 K.,” or “9 K.”)".
"14k" is a word for the metal gold alloy,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "Such product may also be marked and described by a designation of the karat fineness of the gold alloy unaccompanied by the word “Gold” (for example, “14 Karat,” “14Kt.,” “14 K.,” or “9 K.”)".
"10k" is a word for the metal gold alloy,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "Such product may also be marked and described by a designation of the karat fineness of the gold alloy unaccompanied by the word “Gold” (for example, “14 Karat,” “14Kt.,” “14 K.,” or “9 K.”)".
"9k" is a word for the metal gold alloy,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "Such product may also be marked and described by a designation of the karat fineness of the gold alloy unaccompanied by the word “Gold” (for example, “14 Karat,” “14Kt.,” “14 K.,” or “9 K.”)".
"18k gold" is a word for the metal gold alloy,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "An industry product or part thereof, composed throughout of an alloy of gold may be marked and described as “Gold” when such word “Gold,” wherever appearing, is immediately preceded by a correct designation of the karat fineness of the alloy".
"14k gold" is a word for the metal gold alloy,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "An industry product or part thereof, composed throughout of an alloy of gold may be marked and described as “Gold” when such word “Gold,” wherever appearing, is immediately preceded by a correct designation of the karat fineness of the alloy".
"platinum" is a word for the metal platinum,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "An industry product consisting of at least 950 parts per thousand pure Platinum may be marked or described as “Platinum.”".
"gold filled" is a word for cladding with gold,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "on which there has been affixed on all significant surfaces by soldering, brazing, welding, or other mechanical means a plating of gold alloy".

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Gems: stones (natural, synthetic) and cultured pearls. Imitation stones
% (glass, "crystal", "rhinestone", plastics) and imitation pearls are no gems
% of Chapter 71: no word here makes them one.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
"cubic zirconia" is a word for the gem cubic zirconia,
    as stated in ruling HQ H328582, confer "that cubic zirconia qualifies as a semi-precious stone for tariff classification purposes".
"cz" is a word for the gem cubic zirconia,
    as stated in ruling NY N151556, confer "semiprecious gemstone of Cubic Zirconium (Cubic Zirconia – CZ)".
"zirconia" is a word for the gem cubic zirconia,
    as stated in ruling HQ H063616, confer "deeming zirconia a semiprecious stone".
"cultured pearl" is a word for the gem cultured pearl,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "Cultured pearl: The composite product created when a nucleus".
"freshwater cultured pearl" is a word for the gem cultured pearl,
    as stated in ruling NY N339139, confer "a sterling silver ring with a treated freshwater cultured pearl".
"diamond" is a word for the gem diamond,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "It is unfair or deceptive to use the unqualified word “diamond” to describe or identify any object or product not meeting the requirements specified in the definition of diamond".
"lab grown diamond" is a word for the gem synthetic diamond,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "The use of the word “cultured” to describe laboratory-created diamonds".
"lab created diamond" is a word for the gem synthetic diamond,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "The use of the word “cultured” to describe laboratory-created diamonds".
"ruby" is a word for the gem ruby,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "It is unfair or deceptive to use the unqualified words “ruby,” “sapphire,” “emerald,” “topaz,” or the name of any other precious or semi-precious stone to describe any product that is not in fact a mined stone of the type described".
"sapphire" is a word for the gem sapphire,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "It is unfair or deceptive to use the unqualified words “ruby,” “sapphire,” “emerald,” “topaz,” or the name of any other precious or semi-precious stone to describe any product that is not in fact a mined stone of the type described".
"emerald" is a word for the gem emerald,
    as stated in the jewelry guides, confer "It is unfair or deceptive to use the unqualified words “ruby,” “sapphire,” “emerald,” “topaz,” or the name of any other precious or semi-precious stone to describe any product that is not in fact a mined stone of the type described".
"created ruby" is a word for the gem synthetic ruby,
    as stated in ruling NY N073978, confer "This item is a sterling silver ring featuring a created ruby".
"onyx" is a word for the gem onyx,
    as stated in ruling NY N088048, confer "The interchangeable stones include Black Onyx, Tiger Eye, Red Jasper, Lavender Jade, Green Jade, Amazonite and Rhodonite".
"black onyx" is a word for the gem onyx,
    as stated in ruling NY N088048, confer "The interchangeable stones include Black Onyx, Tiger Eye, Red Jasper, Lavender Jade, Green Jade, Amazonite and Rhodonite".
"tiger eye" is a word for the gem tiger eye,
    as stated in ruling NY N088048, confer "The interchangeable stones include Black Onyx, Tiger Eye, Red Jasper, Lavender Jade, Green Jade, Amazonite and Rhodonite".
"jasper" is a word for the gem jasper,
    as stated in ruling NY N088048, confer "The interchangeable stones include Black Onyx, Tiger Eye, Red Jasper, Lavender Jade, Green Jade, Amazonite and Rhodonite".
"jade" is a word for the gem jade,
    as stated in ruling NY N088048, confer "The interchangeable stones include Black Onyx, Tiger Eye, Red Jasper, Lavender Jade, Green Jade, Amazonite and Rhodonite".
"amazonite" is a word for the gem amazonite,
    as stated in ruling NY N088048, confer "The interchangeable stones include Black Onyx, Tiger Eye, Red Jasper, Lavender Jade, Green Jade, Amazonite and Rhodonite".
"rhodonite" is a word for the gem rhodonite,
    as stated in ruling NY N088048, confer "The interchangeable stones include Black Onyx, Tiger Eye, Red Jasper, Lavender Jade, Green Jade, Amazonite and Rhodonite".
"marcasite" is a word for the gem marcasite,
    as stated in ruling NY N101257, confer "semi-precious stones of Cubic Zirconia (CZ) and Marcasite".

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Hair: human hair, synthetic textile fibres
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
"human hair" is a word for the hair human hair,
    as stated in HTSUS Chapter 67, confer "Of human hair".
"synthetic hair" is a word for the hair synthetic fibre,
    as stated in ruling NY N352618, confer "synthetic hair extensions made entirely of synthetic textile fibers".
